>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is psychology a good career in India? Yes, if you pick one of its three real lanes on purpose instead of assuming the degree by itself opens doors.</strong>
    Clinical psychology, counselling, and organisational/research/UX work are genuinely different careers
    that happen to share an undergraduate degree, and they pay, license, and grow very differently. What
    most families underestimate is the licensing wall: India legally regulates clinical psychology through
    the RCI, but "counsellor" is not a protected title at all, which is exactly why the field looks both
    booming and oversaturated at the same time. Building a real <strong>skill portfolio</strong> on top of
    the psychology foundation, not the degree label alone, is what actually unlocks stronger income
    opportunities and moves you toward earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Yes, psychology is a good career in India, but "psychology" is not one job. It is at least three different tracks with very different pay, licensing, and difficulty.</li>
      <li>Unlicensed counselling entry pay sits around Rs 2-4 LPA and stays crowded because India has no legal licence requirement for the title "counsellor." RCI-licensed clinical work and organisational/HR-analytics roles pay meaningfully more.</li>
      <li>Real new demand exists right now: the CBSE mandate for dedicated school counsellors from January 2026, a national School Mental Health Policy, and rising corporate wellness and people-analytics budgets.</li>
      <li>AI chatbots handle mild-symptom conversations at scale but cannot diagnose, prescribe, or manage a crisis. Licensed clinical judgment remains one of the lowest AI-automation-risk skill sets studied.</li>
      <li><strong>The real decision is not "psychology or not." It is which of the three lanes you build toward, because that decision, not the degree name, is what unlocks stronger income opportunities and earlier financial freedom.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your own fit with one small real exercise before committing years and real money to a postgraduate specialisation.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

