            <td data-label="Skill"><strong>{item.skill}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">{item.why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Technical or clinical depth alone does not fully explain the pay gap either. A psychology graduate who
    can explain an assessment result clearly to a non-specialist parent, write a readable HR report, or
    present a UX research finding to a product team without losing the room consistently gets pulled into
    higher-visibility work than an equally trained peer who only speaks fluent psychology jargon.
    Communication and market positioning are not soft add-ons here; they decide who gets handed the
    client-facing or leadership-track work versus who stays in routine assessment roles.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>This is really the whole game:</strong> the degree decides which room you walk into, but a
    genuine <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong>, a licence or specialisation plus the ability to
    explain and apply it, built on top of it decides whether you unlock stronger income opportunities inside
    that room, or spend years waiting near the entrance.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ceiling">How to actually raise your income ceiling</h2>

  <p>
    A fair salary table only tells you where most people land, not where the strongest outcomes actually
    come from. Psychology's income ceiling is not fixed by the degree; it is set by how far past "employee
    doing assessments" a person is willing and able to move.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The clearest ceiling-raising moves inside this field are: an RCI-licensed private practice with a
      genuine referral network, not just one teletherapy app; independent organisational-psychology or
      people-analytics consulting for companies once you have proven results inside one; specialising in a
      genuinely scarce niche like neuropsychology, child clinical psychology, or forensic psychology where
      few people are competent; and building a research or thought-leadership presence, published work,
      workshops, or a credible public platform, that turns you into a referred expert instead of one name in
      a long directory listing.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of these are guaranteed outcomes, and none happen from the degree alone. They happen for people
      who pair genuine training and, where relevant, licensing, with visible proof of results, client-facing
      communication, and enough business literacy to price and sell their own expertise, not just perform
      it for a fixed salary or a platform's commission structure.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    On AI specifically, the realistic path is staged, not a single leap. Right now, the useful move is
    learning to use AI-assisted tools for the templated, low-stakes parts of the work, first-pass
    psychoeducation content, scheduling, and administrative overhead, so more of your actual working hours
    go into judgment-heavy sessions and case decisions. As adoption matures over the next few years, the
    bigger opportunity shifts toward the human-supervision layer: the psychologists who help design, vet,
    and improve AI-assisted wellness tools, rather than compete against them for the same low-stakes
    conversations.
  </p>

