  <h2 id="parents">What to tell a worried family</h2>

  <p>
    This conversation goes better with real numbers than with reassurance alone.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What worries most families</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The old belief that "psychology has no scope" or is only for students who could not get into a "real" course.</li>
        <li>Confusion about whether a psychology degree leads to a stable job at all, given how many different-sounding job titles exist in the field.</li>
        <li>Not knowing whether a specific college or specialisation actually leads anywhere, versus just sounding impressive.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What actually reassures them</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Real, funded new demand: the CBSE school-counsellor mandate from January 2026, the national School Mental Health Policy, and rising corporate wellness and HR-analytics budgets.</li>
        <li>A realistic income timeline: modest early pay, then real growth once a licence, specialisation, or data-and-business skill is added.</li>
        <li>One visible proof step already taken, like a real internship, a shadowed session, or a research project, not just a stated interest in "helping people."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    One specific worry deserves a direct answer rather than a vague brush-off: whether a psychology career
    can actually pay well enough to be financially independent, not just personally fulfilling. The honest
    answer is that the ceiling is genuinely high in organisational psychology, HR analytics, UX research,
    and an established RCI-licensed private practice, all comfortably competitive with other strong
    graduate-level careers. The floor is genuinely low for unlicensed counselling with no further
    specialisation. The degree name tells a family almost nothing about which of those two outcomes their
    child is heading toward; the specific track and credential does.
  </p>

